---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "dragging", label: "Dragging is not included" },
  { id: "decorative", label: "Decorative or control" },
  { id: "panes", label: "Between two panes" },
  { id: "bottom-sheet", label: "On a bottom sheet" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="drag_handle">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A drag handle is the affordance that makes something legible as
          draggable: the grabber on a
          <a href={route('bottom_sheet')}>bottom sheet</a>, the bar in
          the gutter between two <a href={route('panes')}>panes</a>, the
          grip on a row that can be reordered.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which element you write <em>is</em> the semantic. A
          <code>&lt;span class="drag-handle" aria-hidden="true"&gt;</code> is
          decoration — the usual case. A
          <code>&lt;button class="drag-handle"&gt;</code> with a name is a
          control, and you write that one only when something is genuinely
          wired to it. Nothing else about the markup changes.
        </p>
        <div class="drag-handle-demo">
          <div>Editor</div>
          <span class="drag-handle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <div>Preview</div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<span class="drag-handle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.dragging}>
        <p>
          This component is a bar. Nothing in it starts a drag, moves anything,
          or reorders anything. The one exception is not the handle's doing: a
          handle written as a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> inside a
          <a href={route('bottom_sheet')}>bottom sheet</a> dismisses
          that sheet, because the sheet's own behaviour claims it. Everywhere
          else the bar is inert, and that is worth being blunt about.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dragging is a pointer gesture. An outcome you can only reach by
          dragging is an outcome nobody using a keyboard, a switch or a screen
          reader can reach at all — so a handle is never the whole feature. If
          you build reordering on top of one, the reordering needs a keyboard
          path of its own: arrow keys on the focused row, a "Move up" / "Move
          down" pair in a <a href={route('menu')}>menu</a>, or fields
          that take a position directly.
        </p>
        <p>
          ExpressiveCSS ships no reordering behaviour, so there is nothing here
          that supplies that path for you. The handle makes the gesture
          discoverable to people who can use it; the rest is yours.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.decorative}>
        <p>
          A handle draws a bar and contains no text, so it reports nothing to a
          screen reader. Left exposed it arrives in the reading order as an
          unlabelled blank. Hide it:
        </p>
<Code code={`
<span class="drag-handle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
`} />
        <p>
          Make it a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> only when activating it does
          something — and then it needs a name, because there is no text inside
          it to be named by. The interactive states (hover, focus ring, and the
          swell to 12&times;52dp while it is held) are scoped to the button
          spelling for exactly this reason: a decorative handle must not light
          up under a pointer that merely crosses it.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="drag-handle" aria-label="Resize the editor"></button>
`} />
        <p>
          What you must not write is a hidden button. <code>aria-hidden</code>
          takes an element out of the accessibility tree without taking it out
          of the tab order, so focus lands on something that, as far as
          assistive technology is concerned, is not there.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.panes}>
        <p>
          This is the shape Material tokenised: a vertical bar in the gutter of
          a two-column layout, 4&times;48dp inside a 24dp hit target. The layout
          is yours — a flex or grid row of your own, or a
          <a href={route('panes')}>pane layout</a>. The handle is
          whatever sits between the columns; it brings its own width and needs
          no wrapper class.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<section aria-label="Editor">…</section>
<span class="drag-handle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section aria-label="Preview">…</section>
`} />
        <p>
          The handle is <code>cursor: grab</code> by default. A splitter that
          resizes rather than moves reads better as
          <code>cursor: col-resize</code> — one declaration, and the component
          does not guess which of the two you meant.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["bottom-sheet"]}>
        <p>
          A <a href={route('bottom_sheet')}>bottom sheet</a> paints its
          grabber with <code>::before</code> and needs no markup at all. Write a
          handle yourself only when you want a real element in that slot — and
          it takes the sheet's shape rather than the vertical one, because
          Material gives the sheet's grabber its own size and colour
          (32&times;4dp at 40% <code>on-surface-variant</code>). One class,
          whichever host it lands in.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<dialog class="bottom-sheet" aria-labelledby="sheet-title">
  <span class="drag-handle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <h2 id="sheet-title">Share</h2>
  …
</dialog>
`} />
        <p>
          Decoration is the default, and it is enough: dragging the sheet down
          dismisses it, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> already does the same from the
          keyboard, so the bar sits on top of a path that exists without it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> there instead when you want a
          visible dismiss control. It is the one place a drag handle is wired to
          anything: the sheet closes when the button is activated, by pointer or
          by <kbd>Enter</kbd>, so the name on it is a promise the code keeps. A
          drag that snaps back does not also dismiss — the click ending it is
          told apart from a tap.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<dialog class="bottom-sheet" aria-labelledby="share-title">
  <button type="button" class="drag-handle" aria-label="Dismiss"></button>
  <h2 id="share-title">Share</h2>
  …
</dialog>
`} />
        <p>
          <code>.handle</code> is the pre-1.0 spelling of the same slot and
          still works, decorative or wired.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>
          Set these on the handle itself, or in a rule of your own that picks
          out the handles you want to change. They are declared on the
          component, so a value set on an ancestor <em>element</em> is shadowed
          by the handle's own default rather than inherited.
        </p>
        <TokenTable>                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-container-width</code></td><td><code>24px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-width</code></td><td><code>4px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-height</code></td><td><code>48px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-shape</code></td><td><code>9999px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-color</code></td><td><code>var(--md-sys-color-outline)</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-pressed-width</code></td><td><code>12px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-pressed-height</code></td><td><code>52px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-pressed-shape</code></td><td><code>12px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-pressed-color</code></td><td><code>var(--md-sys-color-on-surface)</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-drag-handle-state-layer-color</code></td><td><code>var(--md-sys-color-inverse-on-surface)</code></td></tr>
        </TokenTable>        <p>
          The container is sized to the <em>pressed</em> bar rather than the
          resting one, so holding the handle swells the bar without moving
          anything beside it. Inside a bottom sheet none of these apply — the
          sheet's own <code>--md-comp-bottom-sheet-drag-handle-color</code>
          does.
        </p>
  </Section></PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
